WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.767 --> 00:00:01.634
JAIR DARKE: Oh my god.

00:00:01.701 --> 00:00:02.168
Another one, another one.
Wait.

00:00:02.235 --> 00:00:02.969
Wait.

00:00:03.036 --> 00:00:06.272
[bleep]

00:00:06.339 --> 00:00:08.441
JASON DARKE: He's got
a dolphin in his mouth.

00:00:08.508 --> 00:00:10.343
NARRATOR: Sharks and dolphins.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:15.148
This vicious rivalry has been
raging for millions of years.

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:18.251
Two Australian oystermen
get a firsthand look

00:00:18.318 --> 00:00:19.919
at the aftermath of a battle.

00:00:19.986 --> 00:00:21.187
Oh my god.
Another one, another one.

00:00:21.254 --> 00:00:21.988
Oy.

00:00:22.055 --> 00:00:22.822
Oy, massive one.

00:00:22.889 --> 00:00:23.923
Massive one.

00:00:23.990 --> 00:00:28.261
[bleep] Look at it.

00:00:28.328 --> 00:00:30.730
That just come out from
underneath the boat.

00:00:30.797 --> 00:00:32.532
The moment I saw
the second shark,

00:00:32.599 --> 00:00:36.436
adrenaline started
pumping through my veins.

00:00:36.503 --> 00:00:39.205
I didn't know what
just happened.

00:00:39.272 --> 00:00:42.175
That scared the
[bleep] out of me.

00:00:42.242 --> 00:00:44.177
Second shark was
bigger than the first.

00:00:44.244 --> 00:00:46.379
It would have been 12
to 14 foot, probably,

00:00:46.446 --> 00:00:47.981
but it was definitely bigger.

00:00:48.047 --> 00:00:49.883
It was huge.

00:00:49.949 --> 00:00:51.050
JAIR DARKE: Oy,
they're fighting.

00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:51.851
They're fighting.

00:00:51.918 --> 00:00:52.652
They're fighting.

00:00:52.719 --> 00:00:54.254
They're fighting.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.090
[bleep] He just
stole that dolphin.

00:00:57.157 --> 00:00:59.926
[inaudible] He just
stole the dolphin.

00:00:59.993 --> 00:01:03.430
[interposing voices]

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:05.565
JASON DARKE: Look at
the size of the thing.

00:01:05.632 --> 00:01:08.001
NARRATOR: A dolphin is
worth fighting over.

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:09.436
We do know that
if a shark gets

00:01:09.502 --> 00:01:14.174
the opportunity to eat a
dolphin, they absolutely will.

00:01:14.240 --> 00:01:17.210
In fact, it's a really
prized food source.

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:19.979
Like many marine mammals,
dolphins have all this blubber.

00:01:20.046 --> 00:01:22.449
So that's a good
nutritious meal.

00:01:22.515 --> 00:01:23.883
NARRATOR: From
the injuries, it's

00:01:23.950 --> 00:01:28.588
clear the dolphin was initially
attacked from behind and below.

00:01:28.655 --> 00:01:31.357
He swam straight past us
with the dolphin in his mouth.

00:01:31.424 --> 00:01:34.761
NARRATOR: How did the shark get
past this dolphin's defenses?

00:01:34.828 --> 00:01:37.864
Dolphins are
really quite zippy.

00:01:37.931 --> 00:01:39.966
They can outmaneuver
a shark fairly

00:01:40.033 --> 00:01:45.805
easily, especially if they're
in deeper or open water spaces.

00:01:45.872 --> 00:01:47.874
NARRATOR: To understand
how sharks catch

00:01:47.941 --> 00:01:50.210
these slippery
creatures, the team

00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:53.012
heads to a beach that
offers a unique look

00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:56.182
at Shark Bay's dolphins.

00:01:56.249 --> 00:02:00.119
So we're right now at
Monkey Mia in Shark Bay.

00:02:00.186 --> 00:02:02.455
There has been 20 years
of really incredible

00:02:02.522 --> 00:02:04.424
ecological and
biological research

00:02:04.491 --> 00:02:06.926
on the interactions between
sharks and dolphins.

00:02:06.993 --> 00:02:08.428
NARRATOR: Dolphins
were first drawn

00:02:08.495 --> 00:02:12.465
here in the 1960s by
fishermen sharing their catch.

00:02:12.532 --> 00:02:16.603
Today, this site is regulated by
the Parks and Wildlife Service.

00:02:16.669 --> 00:02:19.138
We have some friendly dolphins
that come here very regularly.

00:02:19.205 --> 00:02:21.207
And we get to see them
up close and personal,

00:02:21.274 --> 00:02:22.609
which is really special.

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:25.945
NARRATOR: No need for a
baited underwater camera.

00:02:26.012 --> 00:02:28.915
Here, dolphins come to you.

00:02:28.982 --> 00:02:31.150
And they're covered in scars.

00:02:31.217 --> 00:02:34.387
So this individual that's
just approaching us just now

00:02:34.454 --> 00:02:35.522
is called Piccolo.

00:02:35.588 --> 00:02:38.057
And you can see on her
dorsal and on her back,

00:02:38.124 --> 00:02:41.261
she's got scars from
encounters with sharks.

00:02:41.327 --> 00:02:42.629
Dolphins make this trade off.

00:02:42.695 --> 00:02:45.832
A lot of the food that they
want most and the easy fishing

00:02:45.899 --> 00:02:49.269
grounds, the yummy fish occur
around shallow seagrass beds.

00:02:49.335 --> 00:02:51.938
Unfortunately, that's
also where tiger sharks

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:53.873
preferentially like to hunt.

00:02:53.940 --> 00:02:57.477
NARRATOR: To protect themselves,
dolphins have a secret weapon--

00:02:57.544 --> 00:02:58.912
echolocation.

00:02:58.978 --> 00:03:00.947
They can send out
a beam of sound

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.917
from a fatty part of their
head called the melon.

00:03:03.983 --> 00:03:06.219
The sound beam
bounces back and forms

00:03:06.286 --> 00:03:09.656
a mental image in the dolphin's
brain of the world around them.

00:03:09.722 --> 00:03:11.057
Echolocation is
the way that dolphins

00:03:11.124 --> 00:03:14.027
have to see in dark and
low lit environments.

00:03:14.093 --> 00:03:16.896
Basically, it's
like radar or sonar.

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:19.933
NARRATOR: But there are
limits to this super power.

00:03:19.999 --> 00:03:22.502
So it works kind of like
wide beams on a flashlight.

00:03:22.569 --> 00:03:24.537
They can only see
kind of directly ahead

00:03:24.604 --> 00:03:26.072
of them or to the sides.

00:03:26.139 --> 00:03:28.474
So if they get attacked from
the back or from underneath,

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.877
they probably won't see
the predator coming.

00:03:30.944 --> 00:03:34.347
NARRATOR: Dolphins have a
blind spot, and sharks know it.

00:03:34.414 --> 00:03:36.783
JASON DARKE: He's got
a dolphin in his mouth.

00:03:36.849 --> 00:03:39.152
NARRATOR: Great whites
use their massive power

00:03:39.218 --> 00:03:43.356
to charge from beneath
in a breach attack.

00:03:43.423 --> 00:03:44.624
White sharks,
you would consider

00:03:44.691 --> 00:03:46.326
more of an ambush predator.

00:03:46.392 --> 00:03:47.460
It's probably
the shark that you

00:03:47.527 --> 00:03:49.228
don't see that you worry about.

00:03:49.295 --> 00:03:52.265
Because a lot of white
sharks rely on surprise.

00:03:52.332 --> 00:03:55.101
They're stealth predators.

00:03:55.168 --> 00:03:57.737
NARRATOR: It's easy to
sneak up on a lone dolphin.

00:03:57.804 --> 00:04:00.907
But the safety of the
pod can stop an ambush,

00:04:00.974 --> 00:04:03.509
even from a great white.

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:08.348
A white shark swims with
a group of dolphins.

00:04:08.414 --> 00:04:11.451
The ocean going
mammals show no concern

00:04:11.517 --> 00:04:14.454
for the killer in their midst.

00:04:14.520 --> 00:04:17.757
Their ability to communicate
and spot their stalker

00:04:17.824 --> 00:04:18.825
gives the shark no
chance at an attack.

